<FoundationLayout
  numeral="VI"
  eyebrow="Foundation VI"
  title="The Titles He Was Given"
  tagline="Every title of Yahuah that lands on the Son is worn by gift — a shared title was never a shared identity."
  description="Every title of Yahuah that lands on the Son is worn by gift — a shared title was never a shared identity."
>

  <h2>The Move Under Every Title</h2>

  <p>
    The case for the Son's deity leans, again and again, on a single move. A title that belongs to Yahuah in the Hebrew Scriptures turns up on Yahushua (Jesus) in the Greek, and the conclusion is drawn that the Son must therefore be Yahuah. First and Last. Alpha and Omega. King of kings and Lord of lords. The Holy One. The Shepherd. The move is always the same: a shared title, therefore a shared identity. This piece answers that move once, at the root, so each verse that follows need only do the work its own passage requires. The answer is a single principle, and Scripture states it on nearly every page — a title is not a headcount, and a name borne is not a nature shared.
  </p>

  <h2>A Title Is Not Identity</h2>

  <p>
    Scripture hands the titles of Yahuah, and even His own words, to men who are plainly not Yahuah — and never once means that they are. Start with the word “God” itself. It is laid on a single mortal man:
  </p>

  <blockquote class="scripture">
    <span class="ref-ref">Exodus 7:1</span>
    <span class="ref-verse">And the LORD [Yahuah] said unto Moses, See, I have made thee a god [elohim] to Pharaoh…</span>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Moses was one man, and the word changed nothing about what he was — it marked his standing before Pharaoh, not his nature. The same word is laid on the judges of Israel, and in the very next breath they are told they will die:
  </p>

  <blockquote class="scripture">
    <span class="ref-ref">Psalm 82:6–7</span>
    <span class="ref-verse">I have said, Ye are gods [elohim]… But ye shall die like men, and fall like one of the princes.</span>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֱלֹהִים</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">Elohim</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">God — the very title of the Most High, laid in Scripture on Moses and on mortal judges who die like men.</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    A title a dying judge wears cannot, by its presence, prove the bearer is the Most High — or every judge of Israel was God. The same holds for the loftiest title of all. “King of kings” is the standing name of the pagan emperors, and Daniel says to Nebuchadnezzar's face exactly what it means:
  </p>

  <blockquote class="scripture">
    <span class="ref-ref">Daniel 2:37</span>
    <span class="ref-verse">Thou, O king, art a king of kings: for the God of heaven hath given thee a kingdom, power, and strength, and glory.</span>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    He wore the supreme title because it was given. That is the rule under every title in Scripture: it marks an office, a standing, a rank — and rank is handed down. A title tells you what someone has been granted. It never counts how many persons wear it.
  </p>

  <h2>Given, Never Seized</h2>

  <p>
    So when these titles come to rest on the Son, they come the way every title comes — by gift. The book where so many of them land says so in its opening line: the revelation is the Messiah's because “God gave” it to him <em>Revelation 1:1</em>. And the Son keeps that receiving posture through all of Scripture. The throne he sits is his Father's, shared — “set down with my Father in his throne” <em>Revelation 3:21</em>. The authority he wields was handed over:
  </p>

  <blockquote class="scripture">
    <span class="ref-ref">John 5:22</span>
    <span class="ref-verse">For the Father judgeth no man, but hath committed all judgment unto the Son.</span>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Even the royal name is written upon him, not sprung from within him. Yahuah gives; the Son receives. A title received from Another is the badge of a servant honored above his fellows — never a second God unveiled.
  </p>

  <h2>The One Who Has a God</h2>

  <p>
    And the Son himself draws the line under it. Risen and glorified, speaking from heaven, he still calls the Father “my God” — four times in a single verse <em>Revelation 3:12</em>, the same word he sent back by Mary the morning he rose: “I ascend unto my Father, and your Father; and to my God, and your God” <em>John 20:17</em>. One who has a God is not that God. Isaiah's First and the Last says, “beside me there is no God” <em>Isaiah 44:6</em>; the Son of Revelation says, “my God.” The titles are real — and the worship tells you how to read them.
  </p>

  <h2>The Rule for Every Verse</h2>

  <p>
    So read every shared title by this one rule. When a title of Yahuah is found on the Son, it is worn by gift — as Moses wore “elohim” and Nebuchadnezzar wore “king of kings” — a conferred honor, never a claim to be the One who conferred it. The verse studies that lean on this piece each take a single title — First and Last, Alpha and Omega, King of kings, the key of David — and show it landing in exactly this way, by the grammar and the context of its own passage. The principle under all of them is one. A title shared is not an identity shared. Yahuah is one, and the Son is the One He crowned.
  </p>

